      <!-- SECTION 5: SAFETY & REGULATIONS -->
      <section class="section" id="safety-regulatory">
        <h2>5. Safety and Regulatory Compliance in Aerosol Plants</h2>
        <p>
          Aerosol manufacturing is categorized as a high-risk chemical process due to the storage and gassing of explosive hydrocarbon propellants under pressure. Implementing comprehensive engineering safety controls is a mandatory legal requirement. Any failure in hazardous area zoning can lead to plant explosions, severe liability, and loss of operating licenses.
        </p>

        <div class="image-slot">
          <img 
            src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/AGBzPMBJDQiwDGny/a_basic_factory_floor_view_showing_a-5JmiyIANCLp4jvOr.jpeg" 
            alt="Safety barriers and gas venting systems on an automated aerosol filling factory floor" 
            width="1200" 
            height="800" 
            loading="lazy" 
          />
        </div>

        <p>
          Essential safety infrastructure in any modern aerosol plant comprises:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Hazardous Area Zoning (ATEX/IECEx)</strong>: Classifying the gassing room as Zone 1 or Zone 2, utilizing spark-proof electrical wiring, explosion-proof motors, and pneumatic safety linkages.</li>
          <li><strong>Gas Leak Detection Loops</strong>: Sniffers placed at floor levels (since LPG is heavier than air) that trigger automatic pneumatic valves to cut off gas supply in case leaks exceed 10% of the Lower Explosive Limit (LEL).</li>
          <li><strong>Deluge Fire Suppression</strong>: High-speed infrared flame sensors coupled to automatic deluge water/foam sprinklers that cover gassing enclosures within milliseconds of ignition.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6: FROM LAB TO MARKET -->
      <section class="section" id="lab-to-market">
        <h2>6. From Lab to Market: Launching an Aerosol Brand</h2>
        <p>
          Bringing a new aerosol product to market involves balancing formulation science, machinery gassing parameters, and regional safety approvals. Because aerosol machinery can be highly specialized, setting up a factory without prior market validation is one of the most common budget pitfalls for startups.
        </p>

        <div class="image-slot">
          <img 
            src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/AGBzPMBJDQiwDGny/aerosol-rnd-to-production-workflow-TJdPeRWmxJwTuaqu.jpg" 
            alt="Step-by-step workflow tracking aerosol R&amp;D gassing trials to commercial plant layout setups" 
            width="1200" 
            height="800" 
            loading="lazy" 
          />
        </div>

        <p>
          Key strategies to mitigate early operational risk include:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>OEM &amp; Contract Packaging</strong>: Partnering with regional private-label contract fillers to package test batches, validating market appeal before purchasing gassing machinery.</li>
          <li><strong>Thorough Material Compatibility Audit</strong>: Performing 3-month accelerated stability tests inside climatic chambers to verify that active solutions do not corrode tinplate/aluminum canisters or cause valve gaskets to swell.</li>
          <li><strong>Explosion-Proof Budget Allocation</strong>: Ensuring that safety infrastructure (fire barriers, EEx-rated vents, gas loops) represents at least 40% of the initial facility capital budget.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 7: FAQ -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. What is aerosol manufacturing and how does it work?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Aerosol manufacturing is the industrial process of sealing a liquid active concentrate and a pressurized gas propellant inside a gas-tight canister. Internal pressure forces the active liquid through a dip tube and a precision valve-actuator assembly, dispersing it as a spray, mist, foam, or stream.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. What investment is required to start an aerosol manufacturing plant?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              The capital required depends on the gassing automation level and cleanroom specifications. Startups must budget for specialized propellant storage tanks, explosion-proof gassing enclosures, automatic valve crimpers, leak testing water baths, and complex fire protection infrastructure.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. What are the most commonly used aerosol propellants?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Liquefied hydrocarbon propellants (propane, n-butane, isobutane) are standard for constant-pressure consumer sprays. Dimethyl Ether (DME) is selected for water-based formulas due to its solubility, while compressed gases (nitrogen, carbon dioxide) are utilized for medical, sterile, or food applications.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. Is aerosol manufacturing legally regulated in India?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Yes. In India, aerosol plants must obtain licenses from the Petroleum and Explosives Safety Organisation (PESO) for handling pressurized flammable gasses, alongside approvals from the State Pollution Control Board, Factory Inspectorate, and local Fire Services.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. What are the main safety risks in aerosol manufacturing?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              The primary operational hazard is LPG gas leakage, which can lead to fires or vapor cloud explosions if ignited. Other risks include can rupture during hot gassing, high static charges during liquid shearing, and warehouse storage fire hazards.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. What is the difference between liquefied gas and compressed gas systems?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Liquefied propellants exist in vapor-liquid equilibrium inside the can, vaporizing gradually during use to maintain constant spray pressure. Compressed gas propellants remain entirely gaseous, resulting in a progressive reduction in pressure and spray strength as the can empties.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">7. How is aerosol product stability tested?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Formulations undergo accelerated temperature storage (at 40°C or 45°C), freeze-thaw cycles, periodic spray discharge testing, internal canister lining visual checks, and micro-leak measurements to establish a reliable shelf life.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">8. Which aerosol products are most profitable?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Deodorants and dry shampoos in cosmetics, penetrants and tire shines in automotive care, room air fresheners in household cleaning, and high-margin sterile metered-dose clinical inhalers are highly profitable categories.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">9. Can aerosol manufacturing be outsourced?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Absolutely. Many successful aerosol brands focus entirely on brand marketing, outsourcing the chemical manufacturing and high-risk gassing processes to experienced Third-Party OEM or Private-Label contract fillers.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">10. Why is professional consulting important?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Consultants ensure that formulation parameters (propellant ratios, viscosity, surface tension) align with physical nozzle parameters (valve cup crimping, actuator orifices). This prevents common gassing issues, product separation, slow leaks, and expensive batch recalls.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- CONCLUSION & CONTACTS -->
